{"id":"f57c6a8d-e4f5-46e2-8504-1b3b8ff2a406","object":"chat.completion","created":1784961697,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_dict = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        s = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[s] = symbol_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\\n            raise ValueError('Required symbol not present')\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    retained_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    removed_order = []\\n    sym_counts = symbol_counts.copy()\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained_ids:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        sym_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        if all(sym_counts.get(rs, 0) > 0 for rs in required_symbols):\\n            retained_ids.remove(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            sym_counts[sym] += 1\\n    all_ids = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    # removed_ids that were not processed are those that were not removable; they are not in removed_order, but they are still removed? No, only removable nodes can be removed, and we only remove through loop.\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(list(retained_ids))\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for solve(case) that implements grammar-guided node reduction. The input case has nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols list. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, respecting:\n\n- Never remove root (depth 0, likely id \"root\"?)\n- Never remove a required symbol (nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols). But note: required_symbols are symbols, not IDs, so any node with that symbol must be retained.\n- Never remove a node with retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, all its descendants are also removed? Actually, if we remove a node, we remove the subtree. The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. So removal of a node implies removal of all descendants. So we need to consider that if we remove a node, all its children become gone, so they must not be required. Also, when we remove a node, its parent retains its other children. The goal is to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes (and their subtrees) while preserving required symbols and the root.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, we need to ensure that every required symbol still appears somewhere in the tree after removal (including the node's symbol if it's required? Actually if the node has required symbol, we cannot remove it anyway). So we need to check that after removal, the set of symbols in the tree (considering only retained nodes) still contains all required symbols.\n\nWe need to process nodes in order: deeper nodes before shallower nodes. At equal depth, use node ID as tie break (stable). And we need to try to remove each removable node (subject to conditions) in that order. \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also note: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that we decide to keep, we cannot remove it. But we process deeper nodes first, so children are considered before parents. So when we consider a parent, its children may have been decided already (either removed or retained). So we need to track the set of retained nodes. Initially, all nodes are candidates for retention, but we decide to remove some. The process: iterate nodes in order (deeper first, then shallower, tie by ID). For each node, if it is removable (removable=true), and it is not root (root has depth 0, but we can also use id maybe \"root\", but better check id or depth?), and its symbol is not in required_symbols, and all its current children are already removed? Actually condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" means that if any child is retained, we cannot remove it. Since we process children before parents, when we consider parent, we have already decided about its children. So if any child is still retained, we cannot remove parent. Also need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols still appear among retained nodes. So we need to simulate removal: temporarily consider the node as removed, and check if among all nodes that are currently retained (including other nodes that are not this node and not its descendants, because if we remove node, all descendants are gone, but we have already decided about descendants; but if we are removing node, we must also ensure that its descendants are not retained, because we can't retain descendants if the parent is removed. So when we remove a node, we effectively remove its entire subtree. So we need to ensure that all descendants of this node are already either removed or will be removed as a consequence? Since we process deeper nodes first, when we consider a parent, its descendants have already been processed. But we may have decided to keep some descendant? That would prevent removing the parent because parent has retained children. So if we reach a parent and its children are all removed (i.e., we previously decided to remove them), then we can consider removing the parent. But also note: if a descendant is required (its symbol is required), then we cannot have removed it, so parent would have retained child. So parent cannot be removed. So essentially, we can only remove a node if it is removable, its symbol not required, and all its descendants are removable and have been removed (or are eligible for removal) and the required symbols are still present elsewhere. But careful: The decision to remove a descendant is made when processing that descendant. So when we process a descendant, we check conditions. So the algorithm: maintain a set of retained_node_ids (initially all nodes). Then traverse nodes in order (depth descending, then ID ascending). For each node, if it is removable and not root and its symbol not required and all its children (immediate children) are not retained (i.e., all children are already removed), then we tentatively remove it: we temporarily mark it as removed, check that the set of symbols of all retained nodes (excluding this node and its descendants) still contains all required_symbols. If yes, we permanently remove it: remove its id from retained_node_ids. If no, we keep it. Also need to consider that when we remove a node, we also remove its descendants, but descendants are already removed from retained set because they were processed earlier? Actually if descendants were not removed, they would be retained, then parent would have retained children, so parent cannot be removed. So the condition \"all children are not retained\" implies descendants are also not retained? Not necessarily: a child could be retained, but its child (grandchild) could be removed; but then the child is retained, so parent has retained child, so cannot remove parent. So we only need to check immediate children. Because if child is removed, its descendants are also removed by implication. But careful: If a child was removed, it means we decided to remove it earlier, and when we removed it, we removed its subtree. So if we later consider parent, we must ensure that we don't have any retained children. That's sufficient.\n\nAlso requirement: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That check must be done with the entire tree after removal (including that node's subtree gone). So we need to compute the set of symbols from all retained nodes (retained_node_ids) after removal. Initially, retained is all nodes. For each node we consider removal, we compute the set of symbols of retained nodes if we remove this node and its descendants. Since descendants may already be removed, we need to know which nodes are currently retained. We can maintain a set of retained_ids. When we remove a node, we also need to remove its descendants from retained set? But if we are processing nodes in order of depth descending, and we only remove a node after its descendants have been processed, then when we remove a node, its descendants should already be removed (if they were eligible) or retained (if not). But if they are retained, we cannot remove the parent. So the condition that all children are not retained ensures that no descendants are retained. So we can safely remove the node, and we don't need to explicitly remove descendants because they are already not in retained set. But wait: If a descendant was not removable (e.g., required symbol), we would have kept it. So parent would have retained child, so parent cannot be removed. So that's fine.\n\nTherefore algorithm:\n\n- Build a dict mapping id->node object.\n- Also map parent->list of children ids.\n- Build list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison). (depth is provided)\n- Initialize retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Initialize removable_ids = set of node ids where removable is True, but we will only consider them if they meet other conditions.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n    - if node.id not in retained_ids: continue (already removed)\n    - if not node.removable: continue\n    - if node.id is root (maybe root is id \"root\" or depth==0): skip (never remove root)\n    - if node.symbol in required_symbols: skip (never remove required symbol)\n    - Check children: if any child_id is in retained_ids: skip (has retained children)\n    - Tentatively consider removal: compute set of symbols of all currently retained_ids except this node and all its descendants. We need to know descendants. Since we are processing depth descending, descendants have been processed and are either retained or removed. But if any descendant is retained, then the parent would have retained children (since immediate child is retained). So if condition passes (no retained children), then all descendants are removed. So the set of symbols after removal is simply the symbols of current retained_ids minus the symbol of this node (since descendants are not in retained_ids). However, we must also consider that descendants may have had different symbols, but they are already removed. So the symbol set after removal is the current symbol set minus the node's symbol. But we must check if all required_symbols are still present. So we can maintain a count of occurrences of each symbol among retained nodes? But simpler: just compute the symbol set from retained_ids, then check if required_symbols is subset of that set after removing this node. Since we know descendants are not retained, only this node's symbol is removed. So we can precompute a Counter of symbols in retained_ids, and check if after decrementing the count for this node's symbol (and if it becomes zero, check if required_symbols still covered). But careful: There could be multiple nodes with same symbol. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, the count after removal is at least 1. So maintain a dict symbol->count from retained_ids. When we consider removal of a node, if its symbol count > 1, then removal is safe; if count == 1, then we need to check if that symbol is required; if it is required, we cannot remove. But we already skip required symbols. So node.symbol is not required, so removing it won't affect required symbols. Actually required symbols are symbols that must appear in the tree. If we remove a node with a symbol that is not required, it's fine. But we must also consider that descendants might have had required symbols? But descendants are already removed if they were not retained. But if a descendant had a required symbol, we would have kept it, so parent would have retained child, so parent cannot be removed. So indeed, if parent passes the retained-children check, then no descendant with required symbol exists. Therefore, removal of parent only removes its symbol, which is not required. So the check \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied if we never remove a node with required symbol and ensure no descendant with required symbol is removed? Actually, we must explicitly check because there could be a scenario where some other node that is not a descendant has the required symbol, but that's fine. The condition is just a safety check. But given we skip required symbols, and we only remove nodes with no retained children (and hence no retained descendants), the required symbols are intact. However, to be safe, we can compute the symbol set after removal and verify.\n\nSo steps:\n- Build children map.\n- Build initial symbol_counts from all nodes.\n- Sorted nodes.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n    - if id not in retained_ids: continue\n    - if not removable: continue\n    - if id is root: continue (maybe check depth==0 or id==\"root\")\n    - if symbol in required_symbols: continue\n    - children = children_map.get(id,[])\n    - if any(child in retained_ids for child in children): continue\n    - # simulate removal\n    - symbol_counts[node.symbol] -= 1\n    - if symbol_counts[node.symbol] == 0 and node.symbol in required_symbols: # but node.symbol not required by earlier check, so won't happen. However, maybe another node with same symbol exists? But we decremented, if count becomes zero but symbol not required, fine. But wait: could there be required symbol that is only present in descendants already removed? But if a descendant had required symbol, it would have been retained, so parent would have retained child. So no. So safe.\n    - # also need to check that all required_symbols still have count>0. Could do:\n    - if all(symbol_counts[sym] > 0 for sym in required_symbols):\n        # proceed with removal\n        retained_ids.remove(id)\n        # note: we already decremented symbol_counts\n    - else:\n        # revert\n        symbol_counts[node.symbol] += 1\n\nBut we also need to ensure that we remove the node from retained_ids, and its descendants are already not in retained_ids (by earlier removals). That's fine.\n\nAfter loop, we have retained_ids and removed_ids = all_ids - retained_ids.\n\nThen return {\"removed_node_ids\": sorted(list(removed_ids)), \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained_ids))}. The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" But the output schema has arrays of strings, no order specified? The problem statement mentions \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" However the output structural schema does not specify order; it just says arrays of strings. The problem might expect removal order as the order in which nodes were removed (the processing order). But the output schema only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids, both arrays. It doesn't mention order. But to be safe, we should return them sorted? The schema says \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, no constraint on order. The problem statement says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" That could imply retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in removal order (chronological). But the output schema doesn't specify that. However, we can look at the public fixtures: there are three cases. We might infer from expected outputs if they are provided? They are not provided here. But we need to produce a solution that passes tests. The problem description: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" And \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So I think retained_node_ids should be sorted, removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the order we decide to remove). Since we process nodes in decreasing depth, then by ID, the removal order will be that order. We'll record the order as we remove them.\n\nAlso need to handle root: root is not removable and its symbol is in required_symbols (root). So it's automatically retained.\n\nAlso need to ensure we never remove a node with retained children. That's covered.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) that receives the payload object with keys \"nodes\", \"required_symbols\", \"_case_nonce\". We ignore nonce.\n\nEdge cases: If required_symbols includes a symbol that does not appear in any node (except root?), that might be infeasible? But we assume valid input. However, we should raise ValueError if infeasible? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we need to check that initial tree already contains all required_symbols. Also check that root is present, etc.\n\nAdditionally, we need to ensure that we don't remove root. We'll skip root by checking depth==0 or id==\"root\". But better use depth==0.\n\nNow write the code.\n\nWe must output a JSON with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source. The source must define solve(case). Must use only standard library. No external imports. No file/network, etc.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) as described.\n\nOne nuance: The input case nodes may not be in any order; we need to build children map. Also we need to sort nodes. Use node[\"depth\"] as int. Node ID is string.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build dicts\n    node_dict = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n[\"id\"])\n    # Check root: must have parent null and depth 0, symbol root likely\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            root_id = n[\"id\"]\n            break\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node\")\n    # Check that all required symbols appear in initial tree\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        s = n[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[s] = symbol_counts.get(s, 0) + 1\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Required symbol not present initially\")\n    # Sort nodes: deeper first, then by id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    retained_ids = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed_order = []  # list of removed IDs in order\n    # current symbol counts (we will update as we remove)\n    # We'll maintain a counter that we modify\n    sym_counts = symbol_counts.copy()\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained_ids:\n            continue\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check children\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained_ids for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # Tentatively remove\n        # Decrement symbol count for this node\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        sym_counts[sym] -= 1\n        # Check required symbols still present\n        if all(sym_counts.get(rs, 0) > 0 for rs in required_symbols):\n            # Remove permanently\n            retained_ids.remove(nid)\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n            # No need to adjust children because they are already removed (if any) and not in retained_ids. But if there were children that were not removed (they would have been retained), then the check above would have prevented removal. So safe.\n        else:\n            # Revert\n            sym_counts[sym] += 1\n    # After loop, removed_ids = all_ids - retained_ids\n    all_ids = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    removed_ids = all_ids - retained_ids\n    # Order removed_ids as removal order (list)\n    # Ensure all removed_ids are in removed_order, but there might be some that were never processed? Actually all removals happen through the loop, so removed_order should be exactly the sequence of IDs removed. But just in case, we can use removed_order as is.\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,  # actual removal order\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained_ids))\n    }\n\nBut note: The output schema says \"removed_node_ids\" is an array of strings, no specification of order. The problem statement says \"actual removal order\". So we should return removal order, not sorted. We'll use removed_order list.\n\nAlso need to handle case where there are multiple roots? But assume one root.\n\nAlso check that root is not removable. In fixture, root has removable=false. But we skip anyway.\n\nNow, ensure compliance: The function must not mutate case. We are not mutating.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation string.\n\nWe need to escape the Python source string properly for JSON.\n\nWe'll write the code as a single string with proper indentation. Use triple quotes to define the function, then escape for JSON.\n\nBetter to write a short version without extra comments to reduce length, but ensure correctness.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2142,"completion_tokens":4799,"total_tokens":6941,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4239},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":350},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}